# Credentials — never commit
*.p8
*.pem
*.key
rsa_key*
config.toml
.env
.env.*
credentials*

# Snowflake CLI local state
/.snowflake/

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editors
/.vscode/
/.idea/
*.swp

# Temporary exports
/tmp/
*.tmp

# Delivered data formats under data/ are never ignored.
#
# WHY THIS BLOCK EXISTS. Both the manifest and the coverage check derive from the
# git index, so a delivered file that `git add` silently skips is invisible to
# every integrity gate: it never reaches Snowflake while CI reports green and the
# documentation asserts the delivery is committed byte-identical.
#
# WHAT IT COVERS, EXACTLY. Only the five formats actually delivered. A bundle
# containing config.toml, an *.tmp export, .DS_Store or Thumbs.db is still
# ignored, and that is deliberate — those names are far more likely to be an
# exporter artefact or a secret than payload, and re-including them to be
# thorough is how the blanket `!data/**` leaked credentials in the first place.
# If a delivery genuinely carries data under one of those names, it must be
# handled explicitly rather than swept in by a broad rule.
#
# WHY IT IS SCOPED. An earlier version used a blanket `!data/**`, which silently
# re-enabled committing credentials: .env, rsa_key.p8, secrets.pem and
# credentials.csv under data/ all became stageable in a PUBLIC repository, and
# the documented re-delivery step is a blanket `git add data`. Closing a
# visibility gap must not open a disclosure one. Only the five delivered formats
# are re-included.
!data/**/*.csv
!data/**/*.CSV
!data/**/*.xml
!data/**/*.json
!data/**/*.txt

# ...but never a credential, even carrying a data extension. Last match wins, so
# these come after the re-inclusions above. A file named credentials.csv is a
# secret first and a CSV second; if a delivery genuinely contains one, it must be
# renamed or handled out of band, not committed here.
#
# The character classes are not decoration. gitignore has no case-insensitive
# flag, and the CI runner has core.ignorecase=false, so a plain `credentials*`
# leaves Credentials.csv and CREDENTIALS.CSV committable. That is the plausible
# case here rather than a theoretical one: Client B already delivers
# Customer.CSV in uppercase, which is why *.CSV had to be re-included at all.
data/**/[Cc][Rr][Ee][Dd][Ee][Nn][Tt][Ii][Aa][Ll]*
data/**/*[Ss][Ee][Cc][Rr][Ee][Tt]*
data/**/*[Pp][Aa][Ss][Ss][Ww][Oo][Rr][Dd]*
data/**/*[Tt][Oo][Kk][Ee][Nn]*
data/**/.[Ee][Nn][Vv]*
data/**/*.[Kk][Ee][Yy]
data/**/*.[Pp][Ee][Mm]
data/**/*.[Pp]8
data/**/[Rr][Ss][Aa]_[Kk][Ee][Yy]*
data/**/*[Pp][Rr][Ii][Vv][Aa][Tt][Ee]_[Kk][Ee][Yy]*

# The directory patterns above (/tmp/, /.snowflake/, /.vscode/, /.idea/) are
# anchored to the repository root on purpose. Git does not descend into an
# excluded directory, so no `!data/**/*.ext` negation can rescue a file inside
# one: an unanchored `tmp/` silently swallowed data/client_c/tmp/Orders.csv, and
# neither the manifest nor any gate would have noticed, since both derive from
# the index. Anchoring means those patterns cannot match inside data/ at all.
#
# CI step 2b probes the OUTCOMES of this block — delivered formats visible,
# credentials ignored, nested directories not swallowing deliveries — so
# shadowing it with a later pattern fails the build.
#
# It cannot detect deleting the `!data/**/*.ext` lines themselves. Those
# negations are a no-op against today's pattern set: nothing above them matches
# those five extensions, so removing them changes no outcome and 2b stays green.
# They are insurance against a future pattern, and a maintainer tidying them as
# redundant would remove that insurance with a clean build. Leave them.
